p1 = [
 "Millets are small-seeded grasses that Indian farmers have grown for thousands of years. Ragi, bajra, jowar, kodo and many other varieties once formed the everyday meal of villages across the dry regions of the country. Grandmothers prepared rotis, porridges and dumplings from them without calling them anything special. Today, nutrition experts have given the group a fashionable new name — 'nutri-cereals' — and the humble grain is slowly returning to the dining table, the school kitchen and even the restaurant menu.",
 "The decline of millets was not an accident. After the Green Revolution, public policy focused on increasing the production of wheat and rice. Farmers received seeds, fertiliser and assured purchase prices for these crops, while the cheap rations distributed through the public system also consisted mainly of rice and wheat. Millets were gradually seen as the 'poor man's food', something to be eaten only when nothing better was available. As incomes rose, families proudly shifted to polished rice and refined flour, and the area under millet cultivation shrank year after year.",
 "Scientists now argue that the shift was costly. Millets are rich in dietary fibre, iron, calcium and other minerals, and they release energy slowly, which helps in controlling blood sugar. They are also remarkably hardy crops. They need far less water than rice, grow on poor soil, tolerate heat and seldom need heavy chemical fertilisers. In a time of erratic rainfall and rising temperatures, a crop that survives where others fail gives small farmers a valuable measure of security.",
 "Recognition of these strengths has come from several directions. The United Nations declared 2023 the International Year of Millets after a proposal by India, drawing global attention to the grains. State governments have included millets in school meals, and self-help groups run millet cafés and sell ready-to-cook mixes. New processing machines remove the tough outer husk quickly, reducing the long hours of labour that once made millet cooking tiresome. As a result, young urban consumers who never tasted these foods are discovering them as tasty and convenient.",
 "Challenges remain. Many people still prefer the softness of polished rice, millet products can cost more than ordinary cereals, and farmers need assured markets before they will risk planting them again. Yet the return of millets is not an exercise in nostalgia. It is a sensible response to the twin problems of poor nutrition and a changing climate. A grain that was once dismissed as humble may well prove to be one of the wisest choices a hungry and warming world can make.",
]
